{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And he took up his mashal, etc.</b> The meaning is: when he saw that HaShem had not commanded him not to speak, nor had He told him the words he would say, he thought that permission had been given him, and therefore he took up his mashal — meaning, to say the words he desired. But his mouth and tongue were forced to speak other things, and this is what the verse means by <b>and he said</b> — meaning, a different utterance went forth from him than the one referred to in the matter. It further intends, along this line, that he took up the mashal that he had prepared to say, but it was swept away and he said the utterance referred to in the matter. It further intends, according to the words of our Sages of blessed memory (Numbers Rabbah 20:19), who said, and this is their language: ״מִן אֲרָם״ — from the heights I was, and Balak brought me down to the pit of destruction, etc., end of quote. And therefore he said <b>and he took up</b> — meaning, he raised his voice in his mashal, which became a mashal and a taunt, for he was from the heights and his glory was lowered." "<b>It may further be explained</b> according to what our Sages of blessed memory said regarding the details of the levels of prophecy, as I have written some of them in parashat Beha'alotekha (Numbers 12:6): for there is a prophet whose power to receive words of prophecy directly is diminished, so that they come in a lighter manner, clothed in the words of parables according to what he can bear — and this applies even to the prophets of Israel. From this we may infer concerning Balaam, that he needed a mashal for his prophecy; therefore it says ״וַיִּשָּׂא מְשָׁלוֹ״. And Scripture is precise in saying <b>and he took up</b>, because prophecy is like a burden (masa) on account of the overwhelming sensation it produces, and therefore it is called masa." "<b>Come, curse me Jacob, etc.</b> The meaning is that among Israel there are two levels: the category of the righteous and the category of the intermediate. We have already written above, in explanation of Balak's commission where he said ״נַכֶּה בּוֹ וַאֲגָרְשֶׁנּוּ״, that one of the intentions was directed against the intermediate — ״נַכֶּה בּוֹ״, and against the righteous he said ״וַאֲגָרְשֶׁנּוּ״, for it was known to them the surpassing excellence of the righteous, that the power of impurity does not rule over them. And this is what he says here: <b>curse me Jacob</b> — they are the intermediate, the language of cursing (arira); <b>and be wrath with Israel</b> — they are the righteous, who are called Israel. And the wrath (ze'ima) is of a lower degree than the curse (arira)."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:13:04.671879040Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}